'''''Calceolaria'' Herbeohybrida Group''', also called '''''Calceolaria'' ×''herbeohybrida''''' [[Andreas Voss (botanist)|Voss]], is a [[cultivar group]] of [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrids]] in the genus ''[[Calceolaria]]'', derived from three species from [[Chile]] and [[Argentina]], ''[[Calceolaria crenatiflora|C. crenatiflora]]'', ''[[Calceolaria corymbosa|C. corymbosa]]'' and ''[[Calceolaria cana|C. cana]]''.<ref name=rhs>Huxley, A., ed. (1992). ''New RHS Dictionary of Gardening''. Macmillan {{ISBN|0-333-47494-5}}.</ref><ref name="Botanica 166">''Botanica. The Illustrated AZ of over 10000 garden plants and how to cultivate them'' pp. 166-167 Könemann, 2004. {{ISBN|3-8331-1253-0}}</ref>

''Calceolaria'' Herbeohybrida Group hybrids are usually 30–45 cm (12 to 18 in) tall, sometimes smaller. They have soft stems and flowers can vary from yellow to red. There are many [[cultivar]]s including 'Gold Fever' with yellow flowers, 'Jewel Cluster' early flowering with mixed colours, 'Sunset Mixed' with orange to red flowers, and 'Sunshine' with yellow flowers.<ref name=rhs/><ref name="Botanica 166"/> Similar to a [[poinsettia]], ''Calceolaria'' Herbeohybrida Group is sold at florist shops and kept in the house until it finishes blooming. It will rarely rebloom in the house because it needs the higher humidity of a greenhouse environment. It does not like summer heat and needs mild temperatures.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=262981&isprofile=0&|title=Missouri Botanical Garden|date=2020-02-25|access-date=2020-02-25|website=Plant Finder|archive-url=https://archive.today/20200226002626/https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=262981&isprofile=0&|archive-date=2020-02-26|url-status=live}}</ref>

